  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({y}^{\u2032}),\\,{y}^{\u2032}>0.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & ={(xp+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(p))}^{\u2032} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{\u2032}+\\frac{1}{p}{p}^{\u2032} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{\u2032}(x+\\frac{1}{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{\u2032}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c>0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(p),$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(c).\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c>0.$",
